++/* Marker on the two session panes (`chat/session-page.tsx`); the blur itself
++   comes from their `mac:backdrop-blur-2xl mac:backdrop-saturate-150` classes.
++
++   That pane is the app's only window-sized backdrop filter, and the macOS
++   window is transparent (`backgroundColor: #00000001`) with a vibrancy
++   material behind it. So the backdrop is not another layer of ours: Chromium
++   has to sample the native material and run the filter every time the pane is
++   recomposited, and the pane is recomposited for as long as anything inside it
++   animates — the working shimmer, the paused dots, streaming text. On Apple
++   Silicon that is invisible; on Intel the pass is the most expensive frame
++   work the app does and lands in the GPU process as CPU time for the whole
++   duration of a run. Intel builds of this fork skip the filter (the 15% of
++   vibrancy the pane lets through is a smooth material already — nothing
++   readable changes), and every build honours the system "Reduce transparency"
++   setting, which is the one-switch way to take it out everywhere. */
++@media (prefers-reduced-transparency: reduce) {
++  .ow-pane-blur {
++    background-color: var(--dls-surface);
++    -webkit-backdrop-filter: none;
++    backdrop-filter: none;
++  }
++}
